{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgKv2B2sEpzCmcgW9zn2h", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I can't give you a current view on Nvidia from here, because neither data source is available to me:\n\n- **Bigdata.com** isn't authorized. Its quick-take skill is the right tool for this, and it can't run until you connect it with `/mcp` in an interactive session, or through your claude.ai connector settings if it's set up as a connector.\n- **Web search** was blocked by a permission prompt in this session.\n\nI can't see today's price, the last quarter's results, guidance or recent news. A quick take needs what the current price implies, and I'd be guessing at all of it, so I haven't written one.\n\nWithout live data, the main questions for any take on Nvidia are these:\n\n1. **How long hyperscaler capex keeps growing.** Most data-center revenue comes from a few cloud and AI-lab buyers. The key signal is whether their 2027 spending plans are still rising or levelling off.\n2. **Whether gross margins hold through product changes.** Each move to a new platform (Blackwell to Rubin) adds ramp costs, and competition from custom chips (TPU, Trainium, MTIA) and AMD keeps pressure on pricing.\n3. **China and export controls.** Licence rules, any revenue-share arrangements and local alternatives decide whether China adds to growth or is written off.\n\nThe main risks are capex digestion (buyers pausing to absorb what they've bought), customers concentrated in a few hands, supply limits at TSMC (CoWoS packaging and HBM memory), and policy shocks. What usually moves the stock is the next earnings report, hyperscaler capex commentary when they report, and GTC product news.\n\nIf you connect Bigdata.com or allow web search, I'll write the actual take with current numbers and sources."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "c